-- title:
--   x and y are non-zero-divisors in W[x,y]/(xy-t)
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and let $t \in W$ be a non-zero-divisor, i.e. an element of the multiplicative submonoid `nonZeroDivisors W` of elements whose multiplication map on $W$ is injective. Form the ring `CrossingQuotient W t`, by definition the quotient of the polynomial ring $W[X_0, X_1]$ in two variables, indexed by `Fin 2`, by the ideal generated by the single element $X_0 X_1 - t$, where $t$ is viewed in the polynomial ring via the constant-coefficient map. The theorem asserts the conjunction of two statements about the distinguished elements `U t` and `V t` of this quotient, the residue classes of the two variables: `U t` is a non-zero-divisor of `CrossingQuotient W t`, and `V t` is a non-zero-divisor of `CrossingQuotient W t`. Thus in $W[x,y]/(xy-t)$ multiplication by $x$, and multiplication by $y$, are both injective as soon as multiplication by $t$ is injective on $W$.
--
--   This is the basic torsion-freeness statement for the standard local model $W[x,y]/(xy-t)$ of an ordinary double point over a base in which $t$ is a non-zero-divisor, the shape of ring occurring in semistable models of curves. It is used in the analysis of the crossing point of resolved Deligne–Rapoport models, both in the construction of the special fibre data attached to a chart table and in showing that the stalk at the crossing point has Krull dimension greater than one.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPolynomial_CrossingQuotient_U_mem_nonZeroDivisors_and_V_mem_nonZeroDivisors.lean

